### [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) QFileInfo::absoluteFilePath() const

Returns an absolute path including the file name.

The absolute path name consists of the full path and the file name. On Unix this will
always begin with the root, '/', directory. On Windows this will always begin 'D:/'
where D is a drive letter, except for network shares that are not mapped to a drive
letter, in which case the path will begin '//sharename/'.
[QFileInfo](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfileinfo.html) will uppercase drive
letters. Note that [QDir](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qdir.html) does not do
this. The code snippet below shows this.

```
    QFileInfo fi("c:/temp/foo"); => fi.absoluteFilePath() => "C:/temp/foo"
```

This function returns the same as
[filePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfileinfo.html#filePath)(), unless
[isRelative](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfileinfo.html#isRelative)() is true. In
contrast to
[canonicalFilePath](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qfileinfo.html#canonicalFilePath)(),
symbolic links or redundant "." or ".." elements are not necessarily removed.

