### int QRegExp::indexIn(const [QString](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html) \\&*str*, int *offset* \\= 0, [QRegExp::CaretMode](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#CaretMode-enum) *caretMode* \\= CaretAtZero) const

Attempts to find a match in *str* from position *offset* (0 by default). If *offset* is
-1, the search starts at the last character; if -2, at the next to last character; etc.

Returns the position of the first match, or -1 if there was no match.

The *caretMode* parameter can be used to instruct whether **^** should match at index 0
or at *offset*.

You might prefer to use
[QString::indexOf](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html#indexOf)(),
[QString::contains](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html#contains)(), or even
[QStringList::filter](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstringlist.html#filter)(). To
replace matches use
[QString::replace](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qstring.html#replace)().

Example:

```
QString str = "offsets: 1.23 .50 71.00 6.00";
QRegExp rx("\\\\d*\\\\.\\\\d+");    // primitive floating point matching
int count = 0;
int pos = 0;
while ((pos = rx.indexIn(str, pos)) != -1) {
    ++count;
    pos += rx.matchedLength();
}
// pos will be 9, 14, 18 and finally 24; count will end up as 4
```

Although const, this function sets
[matchedLength](https://doc.qt.io/archives/qt-5.15/qregexp.html#matchedLength)(),
